      "summary": {
        "en": {
          "research_question": "Maps two decades of global scientific output to determine current priorities and unmet needs in chikungunya pandemic readiness.",
          "study_design": "Bibliometric evaluation of 8,125 indexed articles published between 2004 and 2025, utilizing software tools to track citation trends, author networks, and keyword co-occurrence patterns.",
          "population_setting": "Global scientific literature and publishing records rather than a defined clinical or demographic cohort.",
          "main_findings": "Article production expanded sevenfold following 2013, coinciding with major regional outbreaks. Research activity concentrated within four primary national hubs. Scholarly focus has progressively shifted toward immunization development and environmental transmission factors, while diagnostic differentiation and crisis management keywords have diminished in frequency.",
          "public_health_relevance": "Highlights increasing academic investment in vaccine platforms and climate-linked transmission models. Identifies structural imbalances in multinational research partnerships and recommends aligning pathogen readiness initiatives with broader environmental health planning.",
          "limitations": "Analysis depends entirely on database-indexed publications, potentially overlooking non-traditional or regionally restricted research outputs. The methodology quantifies scholarly productivity and thematic shifts rather than measuring actual disease incidence or intervention effectiveness.",
          "gids_interpretation": "Functions as a retrospective mapping of academic publishing trends instead of an active monitoring report. Offers context on how research emphasis has evolved toward preventive technologies and ecological drivers without generating new empirical signal data.",
